From 59d2ef2f3cdbfa46851dc46005039d34d448e52e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 20:56:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms, nvidia-drm: NV_HDMI_FRL_KEEP, a head that runs over
 HDMI FRL stays on FRL for a mode that TMDS would carry

An RTX 4080 SUPER (driver 620.70.06, with an LG C5) left
the display without a picture four times in 35 changes of mode from the
TMDS mode of a video (3840x2160 at 23.976 or 25 Hz, 297 MHz) back to the
3840x2160 at 120 Hz of the menus over FRL at 4 lanes of 8 Gb/s, on
1.0 (3 of 27) and the first builds of 1.1 (1 of 8): the link trained at the
first try, the display had its four lanes locked (SCDC Status_Flags
0x5e), and the raster of the head stood at line 0 (9072). Read from the
kernel logs of the same days (all with 620.70.06), the other changes of
mode of that output never did that:
- 30 from FRL to FRL (Kodi's start from the console's 3840x2160 at
  60 Hz over FRL at 4 lanes of 6 Gb/s, 2560x1440 at 120 Hz over FRL at 3
  lanes of 6 Gb/s, the same mode again for HDMI VRR or another output),
  whose head NVKMS shuts down in an update of its own and whose link it
  clears and trains again just the same
  (KickoffProposedModeSetStateIncompatibleApiHeadsShutDown(): every mode
  set from or to FRL);
- 39 from FRL to TMDS, at the start of each video.
One of the four hangs came from an SDR video, with no Dolby Vision state
to give back in that commit; the time from the end of the video to the
change of mode was the same in those that hung and those that did not.
What the four have and the others do not is the change of the SOR from
TMDS to FRL, which RM makes in the part of the driver that NVIDIA keeps
closed. With 4 of 35 against 0 of 30, the chance that this split
comes by luck alone is about 8 % (hypergeometric); what it rests on most
is that the steps of NVKMS are the same in both.

NVKMS goes over FRL only where TMDS does not carry the mode, where its
client prefers FRL (NVKMS_MODE_VALIDATION_PREFER_HDMI_FRL_MODE, which no
KAPI client sets), or where the display asks for it in its SCDC Source
Test Configuration (FRL_Max, nvHdmiScdcWantsForceFrl(), a test of HDMI
2.1 compliance): a source may drive a mode that TMDS would carry over FRL.
So a client can now ask that a head that runs over FRL stays on it:

- nvidia-drm: NV_HDMI_FRL_KEEP, a boolean on every connector with a TMDS
  output of the GPU, attached where NV_HDMI_VRR_OFF is. It goes to the
  KAPI as hadesFrlKeep with the head's configuration; a change of it
  alone is no mode set, it holds from the next one. The console's commit
  drops it, as it drops NV_HDMI_VRR_OFF.
- KAPI: NVKMS_MODE_VALIDATION_HADES_FRL_KEEP (an override bit, 1 << 30:
  no structure of the API changes) in the mode validation of that head's
  mode set.
- NVKMS (GetDfpHdmiProtocol()): the mode goes over FRL where
  nvHadesFrlKeepDecide() of nvkms-hades.h says so: the client asked;
  nvidia-modeset.hades_frl_keep is on (the default; 0 leaves every such
  mode to TMDS, writable by root); the mode set does not need the boot
  clocks; TMDS would carry the mode; the display takes FRL; the display is
  driven over FRL now (nvHadesCurrentFrlRate(), read before the mode set
  changes anything: else there is nothing to keep); the mode is not HDMI
  3D frame packing; its format is not YCbCr 4:2:0, nor YCbCr 4:2:2 on a
  GPU that does not send it over FRL (so the format never changes for
  this). The depth is chosen as before.
- Its FRL configuration (nvHdmiFrlQueryConfigOneColorFormatAndBpc()): no
  DSC, at the rate the link has where that rate carries the mode, so that
  the link goes on at the rate it trained at; at the lowest rate that
  carries it otherwise, as NVKMS chooses. Not where a rate or DSC is
  forced, nor with 2Heads1Or.
- The kernel log line of each HDMI mode set (nvHadesLogHdmiLink()) says
  "kept on FRL, where the head ran, as its client asked" for such a mode,
  and, for a TMDS mode whose client asked, why it was not kept.

Kodi sets NV_HDMI_FRL_KEEP in its mode sets while the mode of its menus
needs FRL, so that a video and the way back to the menus go from FRL to
FRL. A Dolby Vision video goes out over FRL in the same RGB at 8 bpc,
without DSC: the tunnel is the same pixels on another link. Whether the
LG C5 shows Dolby Vision at 23.976 Hz over FRL, and whether the hang is
gone, is measured on that card.

A host test of nvHadesFrlKeepDecide() and nvHadesFrlKeepWhyNot() built
against the header of this tree (27 checks, among them every one of the
1024 combinations of the conditions) passes, does not build against the
header before this change, and fails on each of 12 mutations of it. The
five modules build against Linux 7.3.5 with the compiler of the image,
with the same warnings as before. Not run on a GPU.
